course: biology
lesson: 31
title: Turtles and tortoises
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A turtle's shell is not a coat it can take off: it is part of its skeleton, with the ribs and backbone grown together into the bony plates.
explain: So when a box turtle in the garden pulls in its head and legs and shuts the hinge underneath, it is hiding inside its own ribs, like a house that is also a shield.
explain: Tortoises are the turtles that live only on land, with high domed shells and thick legs like an elephant's; water turtles have flatter shells and webbed feet or flippers.
explain: Turtles are reptiles with no teeth, only a sharp beak, and they cannot make their own body heat, which is why a slider in the park pond lies on a log in the sun.
explain: Some do amazing things: sea turtles find their home beach after thirty years at sea, and one Australian turtle takes oxygen from water through its bottom.
explain: Cartoon turtles step out of their shells and walk about in pants, but a real turtle never could: without its shell it would have no ribs and no spine.
fact: A turtle's shell is part of its skeleton: its ribs and backbone are joined to the bony plates, so it can never leave its shell.
fact: Tortoises are turtles that live only on land; they have domed shells and stumpy legs, while water turtles have flatter shells and webbed feet or flippers.
fact: Jonathan, a giant tortoise on the island of St Helena, hatched around 1832 and is the oldest known living land animal.
fact: Grown-up green sea turtles mostly eat seagrass and seaweed; they are named green after the greenish fat inside their bodies, not their shells, and their grazing keeps seagrass meadows healthy, like mowing a lawn.
fact: The leatherback is the biggest turtle alive: about 2 m long and over 500 kg, with a tough leathery skin instead of hard plates; it eats jellyfish, which are mostly water, so it must eat a great many, and its huge body and oily fat hold in enough warmth for it to swim in cold seas where other turtles cannot.
fact: A floating plastic bag looks like a jellyfish to a leatherback, which is one big reason to keep plastic out of the sea.
fact: A mother sea turtle buries about 100 eggs in the sand; the babies hatch weeks later, usually dig out at night and run towards the brighter horizon over the sea.
fact: Warmer sand gives more girl hatchlings and cooler sand more boys, and bright beach lights can lead hatchlings the wrong way.
fact: Many female sea turtles come back to lay eggs on the beach where they hatched; scientists think they remember its magnetic signature.
fact: Turtles bask in the sun to warm up and to make vitamin D for strong shells; pet turtles can carry salmonella germs, so wash your hands after touching one.
fact: The alligator snapping turtle lures fish with a pink worm-shaped flap on its tongue and then snaps its jaws shut.
fact: Painted turtles spend the winter on the muddy bottom of frozen ponds for months, with their hearts beating only a few times a minute, because the cold slows their bodies so much that they need almost no oxygen.
fact: The Fitzroy River turtle of Australia takes oxygen from water pumped through pouches at its back end and can stay underwater for days.
fact: Turtles have no teeth but a sharp beak made of keratin, like your nails, and the rings on a tortoise's shell do not give its exact age, because it can grow several rings in a year, or none.
fact: Pet red-eared sliders let go in park ponds can crowd out the local pond animals, so pet turtles must never be released.
exercise: What is a turtle's shell? | Part of its skeleton, joined to its ribs and backbone | A hard coat it can slip off at night and put back on in the morning | A layer of dried mud it builds itself | A house it finds empty and moves into
exercise: A box turtle in the garden pulls in its head and legs. Why can it shut itself in so well? | It has a hinge on the underside of its shell | It folds its legs into its mouth | Its shell grows soft and wraps all the way round it like a blanket | It digs a hole and lies upside down
exercise: How is a tortoise different from a water turtle? | It lives only on land and has a domed shell and thick legs | It has teeth and eats only meat | It is a mammal, not a reptile | It has flippers instead of feet and lives far out in the open sea
exercise: About when did Jonathan, the giant tortoise on St Helena, hatch? | Around 1832 | Around 1982 | Around 1632 | Around 1932
exercise: What does a grown-up green sea turtle mostly eat? | Seagrass and seaweed | Small sharks and big fish | Crabs it digs out of sand | Only jellyfish and sponges
exercise: Why is the green turtle called green? | Because of the greenish fat inside its body | Because its shell is bright green, like a leaf in spring | Because it lives in green rivers | Because it turns green in winter
exercise: What is special about the leatherback's shell? | It is tough leathery skin over small bones, not hard plates | It is made of glass-like crystal | It has no bones in it at all, only thick fat and a little skin | It is the hardest shell of any turtle
exercise: Why is a plastic bag in the sea dangerous for a leatherback? | It looks like a jellyfish, so the turtle may eat it | It sticks to the shell and makes the turtle too heavy, so it sinks | It scares the turtle away from its beach | It warms the water around the turtle
exercise: Sea turtle hatchlings dig out of the sand at night. Which way do they run? | Towards the brighter horizon, which is the sea | Towards the darkest place they can see, away from the bright sea | Up the beach towards the dunes | Wherever their mother is waiting
exercise: What decides whether a sea turtle egg gives a girl or a boy? | The warmth of the sand around the nest | The size of the egg | The time of the full moon | Whether the father turtle is nearby
exercise: How do scientists think a loggerhead finds its home beach thirty years later? | It remembers the beach's magnetic signature | It follows the smell of its mother in the sea | It follows the older turtles that know the way | It simply swims along the coast until it is lucky
exercise: Why does a red-eared slider bask on a log with its legs stretched out? | To warm up and make vitamin D in the sun | To dry its shell out so that it can fly away | To cool down in the breeze after a hot day | To show off to the people walking past in the park
exercise: What should you do after holding a pet turtle? | Wash your hands, as turtles can carry salmonella | Nothing at all, as turtles carry no germs of any kind | Rub its shell for good luck | Wipe your hands on the turtle's shell
exercise: How does an alligator snapping turtle catch fish? | It wiggles a pink worm-shaped flap on its tongue | It chases them at high speed | It spits water to knock them out | It hides under a stone and stings them with its sharp tail
exercise: How does a painted turtle survive months under the ice? | Its body slows right down and needs very little oxygen | It comes up to break a hole in the ice with its head every hour | It flies south for the winter | It digs a tunnel to a warm spring
exercise: How can the Fitzroy River turtle stay underwater for days? | It takes oxygen from water pumped through its back end | It carries a big bubble of air under its shell, and breathes from it | It does not need oxygen at all | It breathes through holes in its shell
exercise: A tortoise eats a strawberry. What does it bite with? | A toothless beak made of keratin | Sharp front teeth like a rabbit's | Its tongue only, as it cannot bite | Small teeth that fall out each year
exercise: Can you count the rings on a tortoise's shell to know its exact age? | No, it can grow several rings a year or none | Yes, exactly one ring grows on its shell every year | Yes, but only on its belly side | Yes, each ring is one month
test: In a cartoon a turtle unzips its shell and walks out in shorts. What is wrong? | A real turtle's shell is its ribs and spine, so it can never come out | Nothing, real turtles take off their shells to sleep at night | Turtles only take shells off in water | Only old turtles can take off their shells
test: my mate says the shell rings tell u the age like a tree, his tortoise is 30 cos it has 30 rings. right? | No, rings can grow several a year or none, so it is only a rough guess | Yes, one ring is exactly one year | Yes, but you must add ten | No, you must count the spots on its legs, one spot for each year of life
test: What would happen if a hotel by a turtle beach left bright lights on all night? | The hatchlings might crawl towards the lights instead of the sea | The hatchlings would hatch out faster, as the light warms up the sand | The mothers would lay more eggs | Nothing, hatchlings cannot see light
test: A hot summer makes the sand on a turtle beach much warmer than usual. What would scientists expect? | More girl hatchlings than boys | Many more boy hatchlings than girls | None of the eggs would hatch at all | All of the babies would be twins
test: Why does a leatherback have to eat so many jellyfish? | Jellyfish are mostly water, so they give little food each | Jellyfish are poisonous, so it eats them to clean the sea | It has no stomach, so food passes through fast | It only eats once a year
test: In a video game your turtle hero swims in a cold sea and keeps its body warm by itself like a dog. Which real turtle comes closest to that? | The leatherback, whose huge body stays warm in cold seas | The box turtle in a garden | The painted turtle under ice | No turtle can be in cold water
test: A fish swims into the open mouth of an alligator snapping turtle. Why? | It took the pink tongue flap for a worm | It wanted to hide from the bigger fish that were chasing it | It was cleaning the turtle's teeth | Turtles pull fish in with magnets
test: Why don't painted turtles drown during months under the ice of a pond? | Cold slows their bodies so they need almost no oxygen | They have lungs as big as a whale's | They come up every night through holes that they break in the ice | They sleep inside a bubble of air
test: shut up, turtles got teeth, i saw one bite a lettuce. who's right? | Turtles have no teeth, they bite with a sharp beak | Turtles have teeth, but only on top | Turtles have teeth until they are ten | Only the big sea turtles have real teeth
test: Your little cousin wants to let his pet red-eared slider go in the park pond. What is the best answer? | No, released sliders crowd out local pond animals | Yes, turtles are always much happier in a big pond than in a tank | Yes, it will swim home to its beach | No, sliders cannot swim
test: What would happen if a green turtle's seagrass meadow had no turtles grazing it? | The seagrass would grow less healthy, like an unmown lawn | The seagrass would slowly turn into coral and rock | The sea would become fresh water | Nothing at all would change
test: Why does a tortoise need such thick, pillar-like legs? | To hold up a heavy body on dry ground | To dig deep tunnels under the sea | To swim fast like a seal | To jump over predators
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relation of a turtle's shell to its skeleton? | The shell is joined to the ribs and backbone and cannot be removed | The shell is a detachable covering shed annually | The shell is a secretion bearing no relation to the skeleton | The shell is acquired from another animal and may be exchanged for a larger one
exam: It is given that the temperature of a sea turtle nest is elevated. Identify the principal consequence for the hatchlings. | A greater proportion of females | A greater proportion of males | A total failure of hatching | No consequence whatsoever
exam: Identify the means by which the alligator snapping turtle secures its prey. | A worm-shaped lure upon the tongue | Pursuit at considerable speed | The discharge of venom | The construction of nets
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the dentition of turtles. | Turtles possess no teeth, but a keratinous beak | Turtles possess a full set of molars | Turtles possess teeth only in youth | Turtles possess teeth upon the tongue, but none in the jaws
